H2={'datacenters_dyk': "What if AI data centers took over your country? Let's count. Australia: about three hundred data centers, mostly around Sydney and Melbourne. Canada: about three hundred thirty, helped by cheap hydro power. The UK: about five hundred, many around London. Germany: about five hundred twenty, with Frankfurt as a huge hub. Ireland: only about eighty, but they use twenty two percent of the country's electricity! But here's the twist. America: over five thousand data centers, more than the next ten countries combined, and northern Virginia alone is the biggest data center market on Earth! Should AI get its own power plants? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'election_dyk': "Why is election day on a Tuesday? Let's compare election days. The UK: always on a Thursday, by tradition. Canada: usually a Monday in October. Germany: always a Sunday, so most people are off work. Australia: always a Saturday, voting is compulsory, and you can grab a democracy sausage at the polling station! India: elections take about six weeks, with almost a billion voters. But here's the twist. America: votes on the Tuesday after the first Monday in November, because of a law from eighteen forty five, so farmers could travel by horse and buggy without missing church or market day! What day should elections be? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'thanksgiving_dyk': "What if Thanksgiving happened on a totally different day? Let's check around the world. Canada: the second Monday of October, more than a month earlier! Germany: a harvest festival called Erntedankfest, in early October. South Korea: Chuseok, a three day harvest holiday when families travel home. Japan: Labor Thanksgiving Day, every November twenty third, to thank workers. Australia: on tiny Norfolk Island, people celebrate an American style Thanksgiving, thanks to visiting whalers! But here's the twist. America: the fourth Thursday of November, when Americans eat about forty six million turkeys in a single day! When do you give thanks? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'blackfriday_dyk': "Is Black Friday the biggest shopping day on Earth? Let's see. The UK: Black Friday too, borrowed from the US just over ten years ago. Australia: now one of its biggest sales of the year. Brazil: Black Friday often stretches into a whole Black November! Mexico: El Buen Fin, its own big sales weekend, started in twenty eleven. China: Singles' Day, on November eleventh, sells even more, mostly online. But here's the twist. America: started it all, and the name came from Philadelphia police in the nineteen sixties, who hated the giant crowds and traffic jams! Do you shop on Black Friday? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'newyear_dyk': "What if New Year's Eve meant smashing plates? Let's look around the world. Australia: huge fireworks over Sydney Harbour, one of the first big cities to welcome the new year. Spain: eat twelve grapes, one for every bell at midnight! Japan: temple bells ring one hundred and eight times. Brazil: wear white and jump over seven waves for luck. Denmark: people smash old plates on their friends' doors for good luck! But here's the twist. America: the Times Square ball has dropped since nineteen oh seven, and so many people pack the streets that some wear adult diapers, because there are no bathrooms! How do you celebrate? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'emergency_dyk': "What if nine one one didn't work where you live? Here's who to call. Australia: triple zero. The UK: nine nine nine, the oldest emergency number in the world, since nineteen thirty seven! New Zealand: one one one, which on old New Zealand phones dialed just like Britain's nine nine nine! Japan: one one nine for fire and ambulance, and one one zero for police. Germany: one one two, which works all across the European Union. But here's the twist. America: nine one one only started in nineteen sixty eight, in a small town in Alabama, and today it handles about two hundred forty million calls a year! What's your emergency number? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'voltage_dyk': "What if your charger didn't fit the wall? Let's travel with a plug. Canada: one hundred twenty volts, with a flat two pin plug. The UK: two hundred thirty volts, and a chunky three pin plug with a fuse inside! Australia: two hundred thirty volts, with slanted pins. Switzerland: two hundred thirty volts, and its own unique plug. Japan: just one hundred volts, with two different frequencies, fifty in the east and sixty in the west! But here's the twist. America: one hundred twenty volts, so electric kettles boil much slower, which is one reason most Americans heat water in the microwave! What plug do you use? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'vat_dyk': "What if every price had a twenty five percent tax? Let's compare. Canada: a five percent federal GST, plus provincial taxes. Japan: a ten percent consumption tax, with eight percent on most food. Australia: a ten percent GST, already included in the price. The UK: twenty percent VAT, included in the price too. Denmark: twenty five percent VAT on almost everything, even food! But here's the twist. America: has no national sales tax at all, five states have no state sales tax, and the price on the tag usually isn't what you pay at the register! How much tax do you pay? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'dst_dyk': "What if the clocks never changed? Let's check. Australia: only some states change, so in summer, Queensland and New South Wales are an hour apart! The UK: yes, it switches to British Summer Time every March. Japan: no, the clocks never change. China: no, it stopped in nineteen ninety one. Russia: tried permanent summer time in twenty eleven, then gave it up in twenty fourteen! But here's the twist. America: still changes twice a year, except Hawaii and most of Arizona, even though the Senate voted to make summer time permanent back in twenty twenty two! Should clocks still change? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'dateformat_dyk': "Is December eleventh written twelve eleven, or eleven twelve? It depends where you live! Australia: day first, then the month, so it's eleven twelve. The UK: day first too. Japan: year first, then month, then day, which makes dates easy to sort. China: year first as well, the same as the international standard. Canada: you can find all three formats, sometimes on the same street! But here's the twist. America: month first, then day, and it's one of the only countries in the world that does it, which is why nine eleven means September! How do you write the date? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'metric_dyk': "What if everyone used kilometers? Let's compare. Canada: kilometers on the road, but people still say their height in feet! Australia: fully metric since the nineteen seventies. Japan: metric, but apartment rooms are often measured in tatami mats. India: switched to metric in the late nineteen fifties. The UK: metric in shops, but road signs are still in miles, and beer comes in pints! But here's the twist. America: uses miles, pounds and Fahrenheit, and it's one of only three countries that never fully went metric, along with Liberia and Myanmar! Miles or kilometers? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'tuition_dyk': "What if college was free? Let's compare. The UK: about nine thousand five hundred pounds a year in England. Australia: students can use a government loan, and pay it back only once they earn enough. Japan: about five hundred thirty six thousand yen a year at national universities. France: only about one hundred seventy five euros a year! Germany: public universities are free, even for most international students! But here's the twist. America: about eleven thousand six hundred dollars a year at a public college, before aid, and Americans owe over one point six trillion dollars in student loans! How much is college where you live? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'parentalleave_dyk': "What if new parents got more than a year off? Let's compare. Australia: twenty six weeks of government paid leave. The UK: up to thirty nine weeks of statutory pay. Canada: up to eighteen months of benefits, if parents choose the extended option. Norway: forty nine weeks at full pay, with fifteen weeks just for dad. Sweden: four hundred eighty days, shared by both parents! But here's the twist. America: no national paid parental leave at all, just twelve weeks unpaid for some workers, making it one of the only rich countries without it! How long should parental leave be? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'languages_dyk': "What if your country had more than twenty languages? Let's count. Australia: no official language at all, even though almost everyone speaks English! Canada: two, English and French, and every product label uses both. Switzerland: four, German, French, Italian and Romansh. South Africa: twelve, including sign language! India: twenty two languages listed in its constitution, and hundreds more spoken! But here's the twist. America: had no official language for almost two hundred fifty years, until English was made official by executive order in twenty twenty five! How many languages do you speak? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'sports_dyk': "What if every country loved the same sport? Let's see. Australia: Aussie rules football, the AFL, played on giant oval fields. Canada: ice hockey, and kids start skating almost as soon as they walk. India: cricket, with over a billion fans. Brazil: soccer, with a record five World Cup wins! New Zealand: rugby, and the All Blacks do a haka before every match! But here's the twist. America: American football, and the Super Bowl drew over one hundred twenty seven million viewers in twenty twenty five, the most watched broadcast in US history! What's your favorite sport? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'pizza_dyk': "Is Hawaiian pizza actually from Hawaii? Let's look at pizza around the world. Australia: egg on pizza is a thing, and so are prawns! Japan: mayo, corn and even squid, sometimes all on one pizza! Sweden: banana and curry pizza, a classic at Swedish pizzerias! Italy: the Margherita, said to be named after a queen. Canada: Hawaiian pizza was invented in Ontario in nineteen sixty two, by a Greek immigrant! But here's the twist. America: eats about three billion pizzas a year, that's around three hundred fifty slices every second, and pepperoni is the top topping! Pineapple on pizza, yes or no? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'nationalanimal_dyk': "What if your national animal was a unicorn? Let's check. Australia: the kangaroo, right on the coat of arms next to the emu. Canada: the beaver, which is even on the five cent coin! New Zealand: the kiwi, a bird that can't fly, and New Zealanders call themselves Kiwis too. India: the Bengal tiger, with about three quarters of the world's wild tigers. The UK: Scotland's national animal is the unicorn! But here's the twist. America: the bald eagle has been on the Great Seal since seventeen eighty two, but it only officially became the national bird in December twenty twenty four! What's your national animal? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'healthcost_dyk': "What if a doctor visit cost a fortune? Let's compare health spending per person, adjusted for prices. Japan: about five thousand two hundred dollars a year. The UK: about five thousand five hundred, with the NHS free when you need it. Australia: about six thousand four hundred, with Medicare for everyone. Germany: about eight thousand. Switzerland: about eight thousand too, where everyone must buy health insurance! But here's the twist. America: about twelve thousand six hundred dollars per person, the highest in the world, yet Americans don't live longer than people in these countries! Should health care be free? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'billionaires_dyk': "What if your country was full of billionaires? Let's count, using the Forbes twenty twenty five list. Australia: about fifty billionaires, led by mining tycoon Gina Rinehart. The UK: about fifty five, many of them in London. Germany: about one hundred seventy, many from old family businesses. India: about two hundred, with Mumbai as a billionaire hub. China: about four hundred fifty, the second most! But here's the twist. America: about nine hundred billionaires, roughly one in every three billionaires on Earth, and the richest person alive lives there too! Would you want to be a billionaire? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'nobel_dyk': "What if your country collected Nobel Prizes? Let's count the winners. Australia: about fifteen, including the scientists who discovered that bacteria cause stomach ulcers! Sweden: about thirty, home of the prize itself, handed out every December. France: about seventy five, including Marie Curie, who won twice! Germany: about one hundred fifteen, including Albert Einstein. The UK: about one hundred forty, including Alexander Fleming, who discovered penicillin. But here's the twist. America: over four hundred Nobel winners, more than the UK, Germany and France combined! Who's your favorite scientist? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'mcdonalds_dyk': "What if there was a McDonald's on every corner? Let's count the restaurants. Australia: about one thousand, where people call it Maccas! Germany: about one thousand four hundred, and some even sell beer! The UK: about one thousand five hundred, since the first one opened in nineteen seventy four. Japan: about three thousand, and in Osaka people call it Makudo! China: over seven thousand, opening hundreds more every year, after its first one in nineteen ninety! But here's the twist. America: about thirteen thousand five hundred McDonald's, and roughly one in eight American workers has worked at one! Big Mac or McNuggets? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'olympics_dyk': "What if your country won the most Olympic gold? Let's count Summer Olympic golds of all time. Australia: about one hundred eighty, impressive for a country of twenty seven million! Japan: about one hundred ninety, with judo as its golden sport. France: about two hundred forty, after hosting the Games in twenty twenty four. The UK: about three hundred. China: about three hundred too, and growing fast! But here's the twist. America: over one thousand one hundred gold medals, more than the next three countries combined, and Michael Phelps alone has twenty three! Which sport would you win gold in? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'dogs_dyk': "What if there were more dogs than kids in your country? Let's count pet dogs. Australia: about six million, in about half of all homes! Japan: about seven million, and pets now outnumber children under fifteen! The UK: about thirteen million, and dogs are welcome in lots of pubs! China: over fifty million, mostly in cities, and pet ownership there is booming. Brazil: about sixty eight million, one of the most dog loving countries on Earth! But here's the twist. America: about ninety million dogs, the most on Earth, and Americans spend over one hundred fifty billion dollars a year on their pets! Dog person or cat person? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'economy_dyk': "What if your country had the biggest economy on Earth? Let's compare GDP in twenty twenty five. Australia: about one point eight trillion dollars, powered by mining and education. The UK: about three point eight trillion. Japan: about four point two trillion, now the fourth biggest on Earth. Germany: about four point seven trillion. China: about nineteen trillion, the second biggest! But here's the twist. America: about thirty trillion dollars, around a quarter of the whole world's economy, with just four percent of the world's people! Which country will be number one in twenty fifty? Comment below, and subscribe for more!"}
